---
import Site from '../layouts/Site.astro';
import PageHeader from '../components/PageHeader.astro';
import { release, url } from '../lib/publication';
const current = release.claims.find(c => c.id === 'C-IMD-RAINFALL-DEP-india-20260923');
const currentSource = current && url(`/sources/${current.evidenceIds[0]}/`);
const years = [
  ['C-IMD-HISTORY-SOUTH-2002', '2002 · a dry South Peninsula season', 'IMD’s completed June–September South Peninsula series totals 466.9 mm, 34.8% below its displayed 1971–2020 normal. That is a regional rainfall fact, not a record of 2026 severity.'],
  ['C-IMD-HISTORY-SOUTH-2009', '2009 · the national and southern pictures differed', 'The same completed-season series gives South Peninsula 681.6 mm (−4.8%) while all India was 709.1 mm (−18.4%) against their respective displayed normals. A national departure did not describe every region equally.'],
  ['C-IMD-HISTORY-SOUTH-2025', '2025 · a recent wetter counterexample', 'South Peninsula received 787.2 mm in the completed June–September season, 9.9% above the table’s displayed normal. One wet year does not prove a trend, but it matters when testing a simple decline story.'],
] as const;
---
<Site title="Has this happened before?" description="Why India's 2026 rainfall cannot yet be honestly ranked against complete historical monsoons.">
  <PageHeader title="Has this happened before?" eyebrow="History · comparison desk" description="An earlier dry monsoon can illuminate the question. It cannot supply an answer unless the place, dates and measuring rules match." />
  <section class="section"><div class="shell page-grid"><article>
    <p class="eyebrow">The answer as of this release</p><h2>No defensible 2026 rank yet.</h2><p>Our reviewed national observation ends on <strong>23 September 2026</strong>. A completed June–September season includes days this snapshot does not. Ranking the partial period against complete past seasons would count different amounts of time. The current <a href={currentSource || url('/sources/')}>IMD graphic</a> is also a rounded display, not a replayable daily historical series.</p>
    <div class="figure story-contrast"><p class="eyebrow">The comparison test</p><div class="grid-2"><div><h3>What we can show</h3><p>IMD reported 713.3 mm across India from 1 June to 23 September 2026, against 837.7 mm for those dates under its 1971–2020 normal. The reported departure is −14.9%. <a href={currentSource || url('/sources/')}>Source and method ↗</a></p></div><div><h3>What we cannot call it</h3><p>A rank among full monsoons, a recurrence interval, a historical drought analogue, or a prediction of economic and human consequences.</p></div></div></div>
    <h2>Three clocks that a chart must keep separate</h2><ol class="timeline"><li><strong>Season clock.</strong> Compare 1 June–23 September only with that same cutoff in earlier years—or wait for an approved complete 2026 season.</li><li><strong>Measurement clock.</strong> Keep one region, observing product, boundary and normal period. A South Peninsula table and an all-India graphic are different series.</li><li><strong>Social clock.</strong> Population, irrigation, storage, markets and public institutions change. Even matching rain does not recreate an earlier outcome.</li></ol>
    <h2>Three completed seasons, three different signals</h2><p>These are brief extractions checked by a separate agent against one IMD historical rainfall product. They use <strong>completed June–September seasons</strong>; 2026 still ends on 23 September here, so the rows below are context, not a ranked comparison or water-supply analogues.</p><div class="story-timeline">{years.map(([id,heading,text]) => {const claim=release.claims.find(c => c.id === id);return claim && <article class="editorial-card story-step"><p class="eyebrow">Completed season · IMD reported/derived</p><h3>{heading}</h3><p>{text} <a href={url(`/sources/${claim.evidenceIds[0]}/`)}>IMD historical source and method ↗</a></p></article>})}</div><p>For an actual historical analogue, rainfall is only the first layer. We would also need place-specific storage, irrigation, access, markets and institutional records for that episode, then ask what changed by 2026. Those case histories have not been approved here.</p>
    <div class="note"><strong>Next evidence needed</strong><p>A versioned daily historical series for the same geographic footprint; a matching 23 September cutoff; independently repeated calculations; and service or impact records for any selected episode. Until then, the answer is a dated shortfall, not a historical superlative.</p></div>
  </article><aside><p class="eyebrow">Follow the question</p><a href={url('/explore/')}>Current rainfall evidence</a><a href={url('/water-and-people/')}>What changed for people?</a><a href={url('/methodology/')}>Comparison rules</a><a href={url('/data-status/')}>Coverage and freshness</a></aside></div></section>
</Site>
